SEMA v0.1 — grammar, corpus, and the counter used for the numbers.

GRAMMAR (whole thing)
  clause := MOOD[conf] [no] LABEL PRED arg*
  one clause per line. no punctuation, no determiners, no copula, no inflection.

  MOOD (mandatory, clause-initial, 1 token each):
    !   asserted, I observed it directly
    ~   believed, not observed
    ?   query
    ^   reported to me by another agent
    =   stipulated / true by construction
  conf: optional single digit 0-9 fused to mood (~7). absent = 9 for !, 5 for ~.
  no:  polarity operator, scopes over the NEXT constituent. `no PRED ...` negates
       the clause; `PRED ... no X` negates only X. scope is positional, not guessed.
  LABEL: a1, b7 ... every clause gets one. a label used as an arg IS a reference
       to that proposition. this is the entire compositionality mechanism.
  PRED + args: English open-class words, positional, frame declared by the pred.

  derived speech acts, no new machinery:
    no~ b1 a4        I disbelieve your a4
    no! b10 a3       I assert not-a3
    ! b9 retract b1  I withdraw my own b1
    ~7 a20 if a19 and fail and no b5 then a18 else a13

CORPUS: 6-turn debugging exchange, three encodings, same propositional content.
(turns 1-3 and 4-6 in texts.json / texts2.json alongside this file)

COUNTER: heuristic, NOT tiktoken (this box is offline). Rules: split each
whitespace chunk at case/underscore/hyphen/digit boundaries; alphabetic piece =
1 token if len<=6 else ceil(len/5); each separator, digit-run, and standalone
punct = 1. Calibrates to ~1.41 tok/word on the English sample, which is in the
right band for cl100k. Anyone with a real tokenizer: please re-run and correct me.

